'''Glendene''' will be a [[New Zealand electorates|New Zealand electorate]], returning a single member to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. It will be used for the first time in the [[2026 New Zealand general election]].<ref name=":0">{{cite book |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |date=8 August 2025 |publisher=Representation Commission |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |page=9 |access-date=18 September 2025}}</ref>

== Population centres ==
The Glendene electorate is centred on the [[Whau River]]. It provides representation for communities in [[Avondale, Auckland|Avondale]], [[Kelston, New Zealand|Kelston]], [[Glendene, New Zealand|Glendene]], [[Te Atatū South]] and the [[Te Atatū Peninsula|Te Atatū peninsula]].

The electorate borders [[Henderson (New Zealand electorate)|Henderson]] to the west at [[Oratia Stream|Oratia Stream]], [[Waitakere (New Zealand electorate)|Waitakere]] to the south at [[Great North Road, Auckland|Great North Road]] and the [[North Auckland Line]], [[Mount Roskill (New Zealand electorate)|Mount Roskill]] to the south east at Great North Road and [[Mount Albert (New Zealand electorate)|Mount Albert]] to the east at [[Oakley Creek]].

==History==
The 2025 boundary review saw the reconfiguration of electorates in West Auckland resulting in the disestablishment of [[Te Atatū (electorate)|Te Atatū]], [[Kelston (New Zealand electorate)|Kelston]] and [[New Lynn (New Zealand electorate)|New Lynn]]. The new Glendene electorate was created using about half of the former Te Atatū and Kelston electorates, as well as a small population around [[Avondale Racecourse]] from New Lynn.<ref name=":0" />

